{#- map.jinja

  The one data lookup every other file in this tree uses, so the tree
  itself never has to know or care how its data actually arrived: a
  grain (a value pushed in from outside, at connect time) if present,
  otherwise a pillar (a value looked up locally, from files already
  sitting on disk) - whichever one carries a real "lessons" value wins.
  This is the only line that differs between the two ways this tree
  gets run; everything else below is written once and reused by both.

  lessons_b64, not a plain lessons grain - see generate_salt.rb's own
  write_roster comment for why the grain-delivery path carries this
  base64-encoded rather than as a plain mapping (a real, confirmed bug
  in how salt-ssh re-serializes minion_opts for its own shim, not
  something this tree can avoid by writing its data differently).
  Decoded right here, once, so nothing downstream ever has to know
  encoding happened at all.

  gates.<area> is a plain on/off switch per area (all default true) -
  flip one off per node/pillar/grain entry to skip that area entirely
  without touching this tree at all. areas.<area>.steps is that area's
  own step list; common_steps runs unconditionally, ungated, ahead of
  every area. #}

{%- set lessons_b64 = salt['grains.get']('lessons_b64', '') %}
{%- if lessons_b64 %}
{%- set lessons = lessons_b64 | base64_decode | load_yaml %}
{%- else %}
{%- set lessons = salt['pillar.get']('lessons', {}) %}
{%- endif %}
{%- do lessons.setdefault('user', 'vagrant') %}
{%- do lessons.setdefault('common_steps', []) %}
{%- do lessons.setdefault('gates', {}) %}
{%- do lessons.gates.setdefault('gen_scripts', True) %}
{%- do lessons.gates.setdefault('shell_scripts', True) %}
{%- do lessons.gates.setdefault('compiled_lang', True) %}
{%- do lessons.gates.setdefault('win_scripts', True) %}
{%- do lessons.setdefault('areas', {}) %}
{%- set home = '/home/' ~ lessons.user %}
